# Strong types

Prefer strong types over raw primitives. System boundaries (user input, file
reads, environment variables) are the exception — coerce to strong types as
early as possible after crossing the boundary.

## Pathname for file/directory paths

Use `Pathname`, not `String`. Coerce in constructors with `Pathname()`.

```ruby
# BAD
def initialize(dir:)
  @dir = dir  # could be String or Pathname
end

# GOOD
def initialize(dir:)
  @dir = Pathname(dir)
end
```

Use Pathname's `/` operator for path joins instead of `File.join`:

```ruby
# BAD
path = File.join(@dir.to_s, "deps.lock")

# GOOD
path = @dir / "deps.lock"
```

Constants holding filenames get a `_FILE` suffix:

```ruby
# BAD
DEPS_LOCK = "deps.lock"
DEPS_CMAKE = "deps.cmake"

# GOOD
DEPS_LOCK_FILE = "deps.lock"
DEPS_CMAKE_FILE = "deps.cmake"
```

## URI for URLs

Coerce URL strings to `URI` in constructors. Downstream code works with
the `URI` object and calls `.to_s` only at system boundaries (e.g.,
passing to `system("brew", "tap", ...)`).

```ruby
# BAD
Tap = Data.define(:name, :url)  # url is a raw String

# GOOD
Tap = Data.define(:name, :url) do
  def initialize(name:, url: nil)
    super(name:, url: url ? URI(url).freeze : nil)
  end
end
```

## Domain objects at parsing boundaries

Build domain value objects at the system boundary (parser, DSL builder,
config loader) rather than passing raw hashes or arrays through layers.

```ruby
# BAD: parser returns raw hash, consumer builds domain object later
def parse(path)
  { name: yaml["name"], version: yaml["version"] }
end

# GOOD: parser returns domain object immediately
def parse(path)
  Declaration.new(name: yaml["name"], integration: :cmake, constraint: yaml["constraint"])
end
```

## Value equality is declared, never hand-rolled

A value object gets `==`, `eql?` and `hash` from `Dev::ValueEquality::Derived`
and one `equality_members` list — the three can then never drift apart, and
the sweep in `test/dev/value_equality_test.rb` catches an includer that forgot.
Tests assert the contract with `assert_value_semantics` (in every test class
via `ValueSemanticsAssertions`), not with ad-hoc `==` lines.

```ruby
# BAD: three methods that must agree by hand
def ==(other)
  other.is_a?(Part) && sha256 == other.sha256 && name == other.name
end

def eql?(other)
  self == other
end

def hash
  [Part, sha256, name].hash
end

# GOOD: one declaration
include Dev::ValueEquality::Derived

sig { override.returns(T::Array[T.anything]) }
def equality_members
  [sha256, name]
end
```

Members are values (ivar reads or method calls), never symbols; a memo or
other irrelevant internal stays off the list. Equality that is not memberwise
implements `==`/`hash` against `Dev::ValueEquality` directly.

## Never erase types

When the language or type system gives you a way to express a type, use it.
Type erasure (e.g., `T.untyped` in Sorbet, `any` in TypeScript, `Object` in
Java) is a design signal — restructure so the type can be stated explicitly.

```ruby
# BAD: type erasure hides the real contract
const :command_runner, T.untyped

# GOOD: concrete types make the contract explicit
const :ui, Dev::Cli::Ui
const :ruby_version, String
const :project_root, Pathname
```
